WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.040
- Well, coming up, we
actually have some nurses

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.500
and doctors who helped
you at the hospital.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.333
- Oh yeah.

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:06.510
- Yes. So what's
something that you learned

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:07.893
from your experience there?

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.160
- Well, you know, nurses, I mean,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.260
we all know doctors make
pretty good money, but nurses

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.110
you know, so I thought
maybe we could do something.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.030
I think they'll be surprised
when they come outside.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:18.863
- I know. I'm very excited.

00:00:18.863 --> 00:00:19.696
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:21.510
- And you don't realize,
like, how, I mean,

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:23.400
even when I had kids,
like when I was pregnant

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.440
I had like horrible pregnancies
and was hospitalized

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.490
for both of them.

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.220
And I mean, they are like angels.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.419
Like when you really can't move.

00:00:30.419 --> 00:00:31.628
- Oh yeah, yeah.
- You can't do anything.

00:00:31.628 --> 00:00:32.461
- Yeah.

00:00:32.461 --> 00:00:33.990
- They're just like the best humans ever.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.160
- Yeah. It really is.
- This was pre Covid.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:35.993
I knew this.

00:00:35.993 --> 00:00:38.280
- And very nice and very, yeah.

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:40.320
I really enjoyed their company

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.760
and they do, especially
when you're dealing

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:43.650
with burn victims, you know?

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.539
Because that's really
a tough one, you know?

00:00:45.539 --> 00:00:46.372
- Yeah.

00:00:46.372 --> 00:00:47.820
- And I saw how they
interacted with other patients

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:49.590
and with little kids and stuff.

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:50.480
- Yeah.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.313
- So I thought we should,

00:00:51.313 --> 00:00:52.290
at least people get a chance to meet them.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.577
- Yeah. Lift them up.

00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:54.410
All right.
(clapping)

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:55.500
As I might have mentioned,

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.510
Jay's had his fair
share of mishaps lately.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.630
First fire erupted while he
was working on one of his cars.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.510
Then he broke his collarbone
in a motorcycle accident.

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:04.577
Have I missed anything?

00:01:04.577 --> 00:01:06.210
- Well, what I was, I
was riding my motorcycle.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.500
I came around the corner

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.570
and I crashed into
Jeremy Renner's snowplow.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.125
That was a --

00:01:11.125 --> 00:01:13.956
- You cra--
(laughs)

00:01:13.956 --> 00:01:15.760
- But how easy is she to fool?

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:16.593
- Oh my God.

00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:18.235
I was like, you what?

00:01:18.235 --> 00:01:19.530
I'm gullible.

00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.430
You know this.

00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:22.980
Okay, fortunately, Jay is as good as new

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.560
thanks in part to truly
awesome medical care, y'all.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.090
We have one of his doctors

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:29.580
and his team of nurses
from Grossman Burn Centers

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.620
at West Hills Hospital joining us now.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:32.700
Before I introduce them,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.400
I do wanna let you know we'll
be showing a couple pictures

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:38.040
of Jay's burns that may be
considered graphic to some.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.450
So keep that in mind.

00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:42.000
All right, then, let's say
hi to Dr. Peter Grossman.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:43.612
Karen, Alyssa, and Amy.

00:01:43.612 --> 00:01:45.862
(clapping)

00:01:47.501 --> 00:01:51.630
- Here's how.

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:52.463
There you go.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.050
The Grossman Burn Center is probably

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:56.580
the best burn center in the country.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.170
We're lucky to have it
here in Los Angeles.

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.070
Show you how good he is.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.633
He is 104 years old, looking at him.

00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:04.980
He looks fantastic.
(laughs)

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.570
Born in the late eighteen hundreds.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:07.724
Yeah. Yeah. You right?

00:02:07.724 --> 00:02:08.880
It's amazing.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.520
- Walking marketing. It's amazing.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:13.320
- It really is an amazing
place when you realize

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.720
I mean, it's eight days,
you know, it's like

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.340
you know those glasses in about an hour.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:19.212
New face in about eight days

00:02:19.212 --> 00:02:21.750
- Honestly, we said this
at the commercial break.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.750
I thought you were joking
earlier when you said new ear.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.910
I thought you were being sarcastic.

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:28.920
It's a new ear.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.092
- What?
(laughs)

00:02:31.092 --> 00:02:31.925
Uh?

00:02:31.925 --> 00:02:32.970
- You can't tell.

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:34.500
I literally got right up in there.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.580
I was like, what?

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.340
I was like, y'all are incredible.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.152
I'm gonna get to that.

00:02:40.152 --> 00:02:41.220
(audience cheering)

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:42.390
I'm gonna get to that.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.578
But I gotta ask,

00:02:43.578 --> 00:02:48.578
when Jay Leno, a national
treasure, walks into your place

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:50.490
what do you do when he comes in?

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:51.570
What did you think?

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.490
- I mean, you have to stay professional.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.860
You know, it's our job.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.380
So, you know, he was great though.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.210
He came in like anybody else.

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.220
And, you know, we really wanted to get him

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.840
into the hyperbaric chamber immediately.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:05.130
Cause he had facial burns

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.380
and we wanted to get
those, the swelling down.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.760
- Yeah.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:09.630
- So he did that.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:10.680
But then he, you know,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:12.180
I was his admitting nurse.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.170
I was about to admit him.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.107
And then he was like,

00:03:14.107 --> 00:03:15.450
"All right I gotta get home to my wife".

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:17.910
And we're like, no, no,
no, you have to stay.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:19.320
- What?
- And get admitted.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:22.230
And he promised us he was gonna go home

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:23.220
and come back the next morning.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.870
And we were concerned, you know,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.210
'cause his burns were
extensive, but he kept his word.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.530
He was back first thing in the morning.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.330
So, yeah.

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.922
- You left?
(audience laughs)

00:03:32.922 --> 00:03:34.180
- Well cause my wife didn't know anything

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:38.670
about what was going and
wanted to reassure her.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.110
So, I went home for us.
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.670
- Then I came back the next morning, so.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.170
- This is why you've
been married this long.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:44.003
- That's how you stay married 43 years.

00:03:44.003 --> 00:03:44.836
- [Kelly] Yeah. Yeah.
- Exactly.

00:03:44.836 --> 00:03:46.939
- I wouldn't know Jay.
(laughs)

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:49.890
So Alyssa, what, what do you
remember from this moment?

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:53.460
- So I came into work and
you know a whole bunch

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:54.960
of people were like, oh yeah, you know,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.500
somebody like really important
is here, da da da da.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.730
I went with Amy.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:01.500
She was like, come on,
let's go see our patient.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.470
I walked in, Jay's there
with all of his friends

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:06.120
there's In-N-Out on the table.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.520
And then Karen, Amy and I were just like,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.350
okay, let's get down to business

00:04:10.350 --> 00:04:13.410
and do his dressing and, you
know, get him taken care of.

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.190
- Yeah. Karen, what happened with you?

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.780
- So I take the triage calls for all

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.490
of our burn patients as a charge nurse.

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.257
And I got a call from
a doctor and he said,

00:04:23.257 --> 00:04:26.880
"Listen, I have a patient
that's a big celebrity

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.050
that has burns on his face".

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:31.089
And I'm thinking, okay, what
movie was he an extra in?

00:04:31.089 --> 00:04:32.638
You know, in LA everyone's a celebrity.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:34.048
- They were hoping for Brad Pitt. (laughs)

00:04:34.048 --> 00:04:35.370
- [Kelly] No but everyone's--

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:37.128
- No, it's Leno. Don't.

00:04:37.128 --> 00:04:38.397
(laughs)

00:04:38.397 --> 00:04:40.237
- And then he said Jay
Leno and I was like,

00:04:40.237 --> 00:04:42.480
"Okay, wow, yes this is big".

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:44.850
And I initially thought of his cars

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:46.710
'cause we all know Jay loves his cars

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.500
and that was how the burn happened.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:53.282
So we saw him shortly thereafter.

00:04:53.282 --> 00:04:56.220
And initially when I saw him

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.590
I for sure knew he had
second degree burns,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.570
but they looked to be deep second

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:02.610
even maybe third degree burns.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.160
And when you have deep burns on your face

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.450
your face swells a lot.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:09.660
And so there's a risk
with all that swelling

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.160
that your airway could be compromised.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.300
So it was very serious

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.920
and we were trying to relay that to him.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.753
And he's like,

00:05:14.753 --> 00:05:16.200
"I'll be back in the
morning. I'll be back".

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:17.033
And so.

00:05:17.033 --> 00:05:18.017
- Oh my.
(laughs)

00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:19.890
- Off he went and you kind
of felt like you were letting

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:20.917
your kid go and you're like,

00:05:20.917 --> 00:05:22.620
"Bye, please come back in the morning".

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:24.270
And the next morning he showed up

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.310
and we were just so relieved to see him

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:29.370
that he was talked to his
wife, decided to come back.

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:32.340
And then we just, from there,
we got on with his care.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:33.173
- Oh my gosh.

00:05:33.173 --> 00:05:36.150
Okay. So how would you
rate Jay as a patient?

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:37.500
Amy, I'll start with you.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.333
How is he?

00:05:38.333 --> 00:05:39.930
- Oh, I mean, he was amazing.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.330
He was as funny as you'd
think he'd be and, you know

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:45.180
kept his humor and,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:46.470
you know, he really didn't wanna be there.

00:05:46.470 --> 00:05:48.000
Not that anyone wants to be there,

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.340
but he was working a lot

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:54.090
and really was trying to get
home as quickly as possible.

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:55.680
So I think in that sense

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.960
he really did everything
he was supposed to do.

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:01.200
We, you know, he went
into the oxygen chamber

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.580
as much as we needed him to.

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.700
And it's really a testament
to how quickly he recovered.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:06.533
He did great.

00:06:06.533 --> 00:06:07.366
- Yeah.

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:10.253
- He was also just quite
lovely with all of us.

00:06:10.253 --> 00:06:13.170
- I mean, my gosh, y'all are incredible.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.450
That is, it looks like nothing happened.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.160
I can't keep saying it enough.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:18.552
- I'm sorry to disappoint you, yeah.

00:06:18.552 --> 00:06:19.579
- No, no.
(audience laughs)

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:20.549
I'm just, it looks like--

00:06:20.549 --> 00:06:21.450
- No, we thought he'd be burned up.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:24.090
- No, you know, I don't want that.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:27.741
But I just, it's crazy how
talented people can be at that.

00:06:27.741 --> 00:06:29.162
(audience cheering)

00:06:29.162 --> 00:06:29.995
So, Doctor Grossman,

00:06:29.995 --> 00:06:32.280
your dad actually started
the burn center, right?

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.140
after an orphanage fire in Chicago.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.070
Is this right?

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:39.000
- There was a parochial school in Chicago

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:41.100
in 1958, called Our Lady of the Angels.

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:44.130
And back at that time,
there wasn't a whole lot

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.160
of burn care that was well defined.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.590
And my father was working as
a resident in this hospital

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:51.450
called Cook County Hospital in Chicago.

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.090
And ninety nine children and nuns came in,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:56.040
almost all of them were deceased

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.330
and that was his impetus
to go into burn care.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.540
And he became a plastic surgeon

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:06.780
and then opened up the burn
center here in Los Angeles

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:08.820
back in the late sixties.

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:13.461
And fortunately nepotism
was very good to me.

00:07:13.461 --> 00:07:14.751
(audience laughs lightly)
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:14.751 --> 00:07:16.290
- He gave me a job when I came out.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:17.618
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:17.618 --> 00:07:19.710
- And so I was able to kind
of join my dad in practice.

00:07:19.710 --> 00:07:22.890
- What a beautiful story
'cause something so horrible.

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.510
But to make something
beautiful out of that

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:25.860
and help others

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.420
because that could change people.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.470
You know what I'm saying?

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.660
If you didn't have that technology,

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.430
it would change people's confidence,

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:35.160
like their personality, all
of that would be affected.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.280
So Dr. Grossman, is Jay
fully recovered from burns?

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:40.920
- Well, you know, everything changes.

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:43.170
So he's done remarkable.

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.870
I mean, when I first saw Jay,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.150
I was a little uneasy

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:49.200
because when I saw him,

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:52.890
he was a little bit more
significantly burned

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.230
than I had hoped.

00:07:55.230 --> 00:07:59.370
And so you knowing that
it's Jay Leno and a face

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:01.530
that everybody recognizes,
there's a little bit

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.790
of angst going on there.

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:07.110
And as I, as I'm leaving
his room for the first time

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:09.761
he goes, "Hey, yeah, hey
Doc, listen to this".

00:08:09.761 --> 00:08:10.871
- That was an impression
of me, by the way.

00:08:10.871 --> 00:08:11.920
- That was your impression.

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:13.853
(laughs)

00:08:13.853 --> 00:08:17.260
- And he, and so he
turns on his cell phone

00:08:18.268 --> 00:08:19.101
and he goes,

00:08:19.101 --> 00:08:21.798
"Hey Jay, it's Joe".

00:08:21.798 --> 00:08:24.517
(laughs)

00:08:24.517 --> 00:08:25.471
"I just wanna let you know,

00:08:25.471 --> 00:08:29.280
I'm in Indonesia right now,
but I'm thinking about you.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:32.219
I heard about your accent
and I'm praying for you".

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:34.320
And Jay goes, "Isn't that nice?"

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:35.250
And I go, yeah.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.270
And I walk away,

00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:38.591
I go, "That's the president
of the United States".

00:08:38.591 --> 00:08:39.810
(laughs)

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:40.830
No pressure now.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:41.663
- No pressure.

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.353
- I think he did that on
purpose to me as I walked out

00:08:45.353 --> 00:08:47.490
just so I made sure I stayed in line.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:48.600
- That's so funny that you said that.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.450
'Cause my mother does this in
every doctor's appointment,

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:56.310
She will somehow slide it
in that I'm her daughter.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.680
She'll like, yeah, as if
it puts pressure on them.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.000
Like, don't screw this up.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.130
Like she does that.

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:03.237
I don't know if it works, but she's fine.

00:09:03.237 --> 00:09:04.380
- But he's a good healer.

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:06.390
He's, you know, there was a time I was,

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:07.620
we were in surgery one time

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:11.580
and I was having to shave
away some of the burn tissue

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:13.920
and I needed some counter
traction to do it.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.260
And I'm recognized all of a sudden,

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.600
I'm pulling on this object to
gimme some counter traction

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:22.890
and oh, that's the chin,
that was Jay Leno's chin

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:25.440
that I'm using as my surgical
assistant right there.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.000
And I will let you know.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.122
It's all his right now.

00:09:28.122 --> 00:09:28.955
- Yeah.

00:09:28.955 --> 00:09:29.788
- [Kelly] It's all his.
(laughs)

00:09:29.788 --> 00:09:31.290
- That was the thing
that I think was the time

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:34.800
when I realized, all right,
this is more than usual

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:36.000
as far as pressure goes,

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.472
to make sure you don't mess things up.

00:09:37.472 --> 00:09:38.730
- Yeah.

00:09:38.730 --> 00:09:39.563
- I don't know if you've ever seen --

00:09:39.563 --> 00:09:40.980
- That is a national treasure, man.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.930
- I know, I don't know if
you've ever seen his signature.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:43.763
- [Kelly] Great job.

00:09:43.763 --> 00:09:46.650
- But his signature is almost
a caricature of his chin

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:47.760
and I didn't wanna be the guy

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.512
who shortened his signature up,

00:09:49.512 --> 00:09:50.954
so we had to make sure
that we were okay with it.

00:09:50.954 --> 00:09:51.930
- Who'd screwed it all up.
(laughs)

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.300
- Well, we're gonna do a
benefit for the Grossman Center.

00:09:54.300 --> 00:09:55.133
- [Kelly] Oh really?
(clapping)

00:09:55.133 --> 00:09:56.505
- To help kids.

00:09:56.505 --> 00:09:58.413
- Yeah.

00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:03.060
- So Jay, what do you
wanna say to your team?

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:05.230
- I think we have a little
something for you guys as well.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:06.141
- Okay. Okay.

00:10:06.141 --> 00:10:07.590
- Just a way to say thank you.

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:08.423
- All right.

00:10:08.423 --> 00:10:09.960
So, but first I believe your friends here

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.480
brought you something that
you don't know about yet, so.

00:10:12.480 --> 00:10:13.313
- Oh.

00:10:13.313 --> 00:10:14.820
- Yep.
(laughs)

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:16.730
And it came in a lovely pot.

00:10:16.730 --> 00:10:17.850
- So cute.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:19.770
So Jay, we love you.

00:10:19.770 --> 00:10:20.603
- Yes.

00:10:20.603 --> 00:10:22.170
- And we don't want you
to get hurt anymore.

00:10:22.170 --> 00:10:23.637
- All right. I can see this coming.

00:10:23.637 --> 00:10:24.669
Yes, yes.

00:10:24.669 --> 00:10:26.330
- So, we'd like to give this to you.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:27.163
- Okay, well thank you.

00:10:27.163 --> 00:10:28.354
- I love that it came in there.

00:10:28.354 --> 00:10:29.850
(laughs)

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.027
- I hope it's lasagna.

00:10:31.027 --> 00:10:32.760
(laughs)

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:33.840
Oh, what is this?

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.210
Oh, it's a, please handle with--

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:37.230
- [Kelly] It says fragile?
(laughs)

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:38.760
- Here we go.

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:39.600
Alright, well there you go.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.680
- [Kelly] Please handle with care.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:41.755
- Thank you. Thank you.
(clapping)

00:10:41.755 --> 00:10:43.275
- [Kelly] I love it.

00:10:43.275 --> 00:10:47.520
- All right, now I, all right, all right.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:51.090
To return the favor, I have
something for you guys.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:52.110
- Okay, cool.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:53.520
We are all grateful

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:55.230
to the nurses at Grossman Burn Centers

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.525
who helped Jay, a national
freaking treasure, heal.

00:10:58.525 --> 00:10:59.461
(audience cheers)

00:10:59.461 --> 00:11:00.810
We love him.

00:11:00.810 --> 00:11:02.640
So, Karen, Alyssa and Amy.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.800
That's why we are sending
you each plus a guest

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:08.153
on a four night trip to
Atlantis in The Bahamas.

00:11:08.153 --> 00:11:09.380
- Oh my God.
(audience cheers louder)

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.030
Which is celebrating twenty
five years this year.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.461
It's a beautiful place. Okay?

00:11:14.461 --> 00:11:16.432
You'll experience
luxurious accommodations.

00:11:16.432 --> 00:11:17.850
Fourteen unique pools.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:20.040
Dine at world renowned restaurants.

00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:22.660
Enjoy white sand beaches,
live entertainment.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.720
Plus, you'll get to
see the world's largest

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.340
open air marine habitat.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:27.173
It's so cool.

00:11:30.392 --> 00:11:31.225
- Thank you.
(audience cheers)

00:11:31.225 --> 00:11:32.602
- Thank you guys so much.

00:11:32.602 --> 00:11:34.254
What you do it's so wonderful.

